--TEST--
`Core\Program::implementing<T>()` refuses an implementor whose constructor takes a required argument, and names the class rather than the call — `rule:programs/implementing`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 3: "each such class needs a no-argument constructor; a diagnostic names any
// that does not, and dependencies arrive through the interface's own methods
// instead". The expansion is an array literal of `new` expressions, so a class
// the call site would have to write `new App\Needy(8080)` for is not one it can
// enumerate at all.
autoload 'App' from './modules';

// Both sides of the bound in one file, which is what makes this more than a
// restatement of the error. The refused test is `required() > 0`, not "declares
// a constructor" and not "takes a parameter": `App\Ambient` declares one whose
// only parameter is *defaulted*, sorts before `App\Needy` in § 3's
// name-ordered walk, and is therefore examined and accepted before the refusal
// below is reached. A rule reading the parameter count instead would name both
// classes and still look right on the second half alone.
var $found = Core\Program::implementing<App\Module>();

echo "unreachable ", Core\Arr::count($found), "\n";
--FILE modules/Module.nvs--
<?nvs
namespace App;

interface Module
{
    public function tag(): string;
}
--FILE modules/Ambient.nvs--
<?nvs
namespace App;

// Accepted: a defaulted parameter is not a required one, so the enumeration can
// still write `new App\Ambient()`.
class Ambient implements Module
{
    public function constructor(int $port = 8080) {}

    public function tag(): string
    {
        return "ambient";
    }
}
--FILE modules/Needy.nvs--
<?nvs
namespace App;

// Refused: the enumeration has no argument to supply, and § 3's answer is that
// the dependency arrives through `Module`'s own methods instead.
class Needy implements Module
{
    public function constructor(int $port) {}

    public function tag(): string
    {
        return "needy";
    }
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0744]: `App\Needy` implements `App\Module` and its constructor takes 1 required argument(s)
   --> case.nvs:16:14
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
